WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:08.573
Sources, rescue and corridors all rest on one record: which ponds are occupied.

2
00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:11.513
Three visits, no toads heard.

3
00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:13.893
Is the pond empty?

4
00:00:15.153 --> 00:00:17.693
A survey sheet would simply score it as a zero.

5
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:21.307
A zero on a survey sheet has two meanings.

6
00:00:22.627 --> 00:00:24.267
The pond may truly be empty.

7
00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:28.867
Or toads were there, and the observer missed them on every visit.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.250
So surveyors return to each site several times in one season, while occupancy is

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.730
assumed not to change.

10
00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.390
Each visit scores a one for detected, and a zero for not.

11
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:46.913
Three visits give a history, like 110, or 010.

12
00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:52.873
A 010 history shows a pond that was occupied, and missed twice.

13
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.313
Take eight ponds that really hold toads.

14
00:00:57.473 --> 00:01:02.453
Each visit detects them with a probability of one half, independently of the other

15
00:01:02.453 --> 00:01:06.693
visits. After three visits, how many ponds look empty?

16
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.830
About one, on average.

17
00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.950
With independent visits, three misses in a row happen one time in eight.

18
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.530
Each extra visit halves that chance again.

19
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.000
Harder species are missed more often.

20
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:34.200
When each visit finds them only 3 times in 10, about 1 occupied pond in 3 comes out

21
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.200
all zeros.

22
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:40.073
No single site can settle it, but many sites together can.

23
00:01:41.673 --> 00:01:44.353
Across them, the pattern of histories carries the answer.

24
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:50.173
A model estimates the chance of detection and the fraction occupied, together.

25
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:54.497
A naive count lights only the ponds with a detection.

26
00:01:55.857 --> 00:02:00.037
The corrected estimate is higher, because some silent ponds hold toads.

27
00:02:01.257 --> 00:02:05.117
Treat every silent pond as empty, and occupancy is counted low.

28
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.220
Darryl MacKenzie and colleagues published this method.

29
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.420
They tested it on volunteer surveys of wetlands in Maryland, made two years earlier.

30
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.560
American toads were detected at least once at only about a third of the wetlands

31
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.080
analysed. The model put the fraction occupied near one half.

32
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:29.203
The method has rules of its own.

33
00:02:30.443 --> 00:02:33.363
Occupancy must stay fixed across a season's visits.

34
00:02:34.963 --> 00:02:37.303
Toads must never be recorded where they are absent.

35
00:02:38.603 --> 00:02:42.463
And detection must be independent among sites and among visits.

36
00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:47.207
So a zero is a result of looking, not proof of absence.

37
00:02:48.227 --> 00:02:52.987
How hard you looked belongs in the analysis, before any pond is called empty.
